theory.intro:
Most figures are several shapes joined, so no single property finishes them. Work one angle or length at a time, choosing whichever property fits, and write the reason beside every line — in geometry the reason is part of the answer.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\coordinate (A) at (-2.8,1.9);
\coordinate (B) at (2.9,1.3);
\coordinate (C) at (2.2,-1.7);
\coordinate (D) at (-2.6,-1.4);
\draw (A)--(B)--(C)--(D)--cycle;
\draw (B)--(D);
\node[above left] at (A) {$A$};
\node[above right] at (B) {$B$};
\node[below right] at (C) {$C$};
\node[below left] at (D) {$D$};
\node[font=\small] at (-1.3,0.85) {$\triangle ABD$};
\node[font=\small] at (0.9,-0.9) {$\triangle BCD$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

facts:
An **exterior angle** of a triangle equals the sum of the two interior opposite angles.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{angles,quotes}
\coordinate (B) at (-2.5,0);
\coordinate (C) at (1.5,0);
\coordinate (D) at (3.2,0);
\coordinate (A) at (-0.8,2.2);
\draw (B)--(D);
\draw (B)--(A)--(C);
\node[below left] at (B) {$B$};
\node[below] at (C) {$C$};
\node[below right] at (D) {$D$};
\node[above] at (A) {$A$};
\pic[draw,"$x$",angle radius=0.75cm,angle eccentricity=1.45] {angle=C--B--A};
\pic[draw,"$y$",angle radius=0.7cm,angle eccentricity=1.45] {angle=B--A--C};
\pic[draw,"$z$",angle radius=0.9cm,angle eccentricity=1.35] {angle=D--C--A};
\node[font=\small,anchor=west] at (2.9,2.2) {exterior angle};
\draw[->] (2.95,2.0) -- (2.45,0.62);
\node at (-0.5,-1.1) {$x+y=z$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

